What is a ConfigMap?


A ConfigMap in Kubernetes is used to store non-sensitive configuration data in key-value pairs.

It allows you to separate configuration from application code


Simple Definition

ConfigMap = External configuration storage for your app


Why Use ConfigMap?

Avoid hardcoding config in code

Easily update configuration without rebuilding image

Reuse config across multiple pods

Decouple app & environment


Real-Time Examples

Use Case				Example

App config				DB_HOST, DB_PORT

Feature flags			ENABLE_CACHE=true

Config files			nginx.conf

Environment values		DEBUG=false


ConfigMap YAML Example

apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: app-config
  namespace: dev
data:
  DB_HOST: "postgres-service"
  DB_PORT: "5432"
  DEBUG: "true"


Pod Using ConfigMap (Env Example)


apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: config-demo-pod
  namespace: dev
spec:
  containers:
  - name: demo-container
    image: nginx
    env:
    - name: DB_HOST
      valueFrom:
        configMapKeyRef:
          name: app-config
          key: DB_HOST


Important Commands

Create ConfigMap

kubectl apply -f configmap.yaml

OR

kubectl create configmap app-config --from-literal=DB_HOST=postgres


Get ConfigMaps

kubectl get configmaps

Describe ConfigMap

kubectl describe configmap app-config

View YAML

kubectl get configmap app-config -o yaml

Delete ConfigMap

kubectl delete configmap app-config


Check inside Pod

kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- env | grep DB_HOST

OR (for volume)

kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- ls /etc/config


Advantages

	✔ Easy configuration management
	✔ No image rebuild needed
	✔ Reusable across pods
	✔ Supports env & files


🔹 Limitations
	
	Not for sensitive data
	Needs pod restart (env vars case)
	Size limit (~1MB)
